# Build output. Regenerated from source; several gigabytes.
#
# `target*` and not just `/target/`, at any depth: every parallel worktree needs its own
# CARGO_TARGET_DIR (cargo's unit hash omits the worktree path, so sharing one makes worktrees link
# against each other's crates), and the obvious name for it is a variation on `target`. One named
# `target-agent` sat outside `/target/` and a `git add -A` swallowed 9,324 build artifacts in a
# single commit. Caught before it was pushed, but the next one might not be.
target/
target-*/
**/*.rs.bk

# Worlds. A .wld is a save file, not source: they are large, binary, and personal, and a stray
# commit of one puts somebody's world into the history permanently. `/worlds/` is the server's own
# world directory when it is run from the repo root.
*.wld
*.wld.bak*
*.twld
/worlds/

# Local configuration, which holds a listen address and a world path for one machine.
/terrustia.toml

# Editor and OS noise.
.DS_Store
*.swp
*~
.idea/
.vscode/

# Python bytecode cache from tools/*.py — regenerated, not source.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Scratch space for profiling output and captured logs.
/scratch/
*.log
perf.data*
flamegraph.svg

# Decompiled game source, if a generator is ever run with the tree inside this directory.
#
# `.gitattributes` already anticipates `.cs` files arriving; nothing stopped `git add` from
# swallowing the whole decompiled tree along with them. The generators take the tree as a CLI
# argument and never look for it in here, so this is a safety net rather than a workflow.
*.cs
/Terraria*/
/decompiled/
.scratch/

# Agent worktrees used for concurrent fork work: not part of the tree itself, and `git add -A`
# will otherwise swallow them whole as embedded repositories. Custom subagent definitions live in
# .claude/agents/ and are real project tooling, tracked despite the rest of .claude/ being ignored.
/.claude/*
!/.claude/agents/

# Playwright output: traces and screenshots from a failed panel run.
crates/terrustia/web-panel/test-results/
crates/terrustia/web-panel/playwright-report/
